{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "sarah-laugh",
  "text": "She laughed at God from behind a tent flap, lied about it, got caught, and still got the promise.\n\nGenesis eighteen. Three visitors come to Abraham's tent in the heat of the day. Big hospitality scramble. And one of them says, \"Lo, Sarah thy wife shall have a son.\"\n\nAnd Sarah is listening at the tent door behind him.\n\nThe text is blunt about the situation. They are old. Well stricken in age. It says plainly that it had ceased to be with Sarah after the manner of women.\n\nSo she laughed within herself. Not out loud. Inside. And said, \"After I am waxed old shall I have pleasure, my lord being old also?\"\n\nAnd then the Lord says to Abraham, \"Wherefore did Sarah laugh?\"\n\nCaught. Instantly. Laughing silently, inside her own head, behind a tent flap.\n\nAnd then, \"Is any thing too hard for the Lord?\"\n\nAnd Sarah, understandably, panics. \"I laughed not.\" Because she was afraid.\n\nAnd he says, gently and immovably, \"Nay, but thou didst laugh.\"\n\nHe does not withdraw the promise. He does not scold her out of the story.\n\nGenesis twenty-one, she has the boy. Names him Isaac, which means laughter. And says, \"God hath made me to laugh.\"",
